rs) match the known distance to Mars/Venus. If it were a simulation on Earth, the signal would have near-instantaneous latency. - Also, the rovers on Mars have been operating for years, taking daily images that show changing seasons, dust accumulation, and even eclipses (Phobos crossing the Sun). No simulation could generate years of consistent, uncannily accurate astronomical data that matches what orbiters see. ### 4. The "simulation" would be more expensive than the real thing - To fake a Mars landing convincingly for decades, you'd need to build a full-scale Martian surface in a desert (with the exact same light, gravity, and atmospheric conditions), launch fake rockets that appear to go to space but actually land somewhere remote, and maintain the illusion for 50+ years across multiple countries. That would cost far more than actually going to Mars. ### 5. The simplest explanation is the truth - Occam's razor: The evidence that spacecraft have landed on other planets is consistent, redundant, and independently verified. The "all a simulation" theory requires an impossibly vast, decades-long conspiracy involving thousands of scientists from hostile nations—none of whom have ever leaked a credible contradiction. I'm not trying to force you to believe. Doubt is healthy. But the evidence for real landings is far stronger than for any alternative. If you'd like, I can point you to specific NASA websites where you can see the raw, unprocessed images from the Mars rovers, including those taken just hours after landing—no Photoshop, no simulation. What would it take to convince you?
